$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: usm/gen-technical-design
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-08-28"
summary: >-
  Technical Design Document generator — renders a 13-section detailed design document from .usm
  specs. The universal "how is this system designed end-to-end" reading path. Each section is its
  own page, rendered only when data exists. Adapts to any project type (CLI tool, SaaS platform,
  library, monorepo). Adds new schema fields for stakeholders, assumptions, personas, non-functional
  requirements, testing strategy, and backup/recovery. Restructures the generated sidebar into five
  groups: Getting Started, Design (13 pages), Project Management (roadmap, features, decisions),
  Developers (source map, coverage, references), Exports (TOGAF, ArchiMate).
$system: usm/system
$service: usm/cli
intent: >-
  Every engineering team needs a detailed design document that ties the whole system together —
  project overview, requirements, architecture, modules, database, API, security, deployment,
  testing, maintenance, backup, risks, and future plans. USM specs already contain most of this
  data, but it's scattered across feature docs, TOGAF phases, and service pages. This generator
  assembles it into one coherent reading path that works for any project type. It follows the first
  principle that generators are renderers, not authors — structured data from specs drives every
  section, with optional content-block enrichment from system.usm for prose that doesn't fit
  structured fields.
status: built
flows:
  - id: gen-design-doc
    name: Generate the 13-section technical design document
    description: >-
      usm generate runs the technical-design generator. It reads all .usm files (system, services,
      features, data) and renders up to 13 pages under .usm-workspace/docs/design/. Each page is
      only rendered if its data source exists. The generator then updates the sidebar config with
      the Design group containing only the rendered pages.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: parse
        target: system.usm + all service, feature, and data .usm files
      - id: s2
        action: determine
        target: which of the 13 sections have data
      - id: s3
        action: generate
        target: one markdown page per section that has data, to .usm-workspace/docs/design/
      - id: s4
        action: enrich
        target: sections with inline content blocks declared on system.usm (if any)
      - id: s5
        action: update
        target: sidebar config with Design group containing only rendered pages
  - id: render-project-overview
    name: Render Project Overview page
    description: >-
      Section 1. Reads system.identity (name, domain, repository), system.summary, system.roles (as
      stakeholders/personas), new system.stakeholders[], new system.assumptions[], and system.index
      (features as use-cases). Renders project name, description, stakeholders table, assumptions
      list, and core use-cases summary.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: system.identity for name, domain, repository
      - id: s2
        action: read
        target: system.summary for project description
      - id: s3
        action: read
        target: system.roles and system.stakeholders for stakeholder table
      - id: s4
        action: read
        target: system.assumptions for assumptions list
      - id: s5
        action: read
        target: system.index to summarise core features as use-cases
      - id: s6
        action: render
        target: markdown page with escaped prose, tables, and content-block enrichment
  - id: render-requirements
    name: Render Requirements page
    description: >-
      Section 2. Functional requirements from features (summary + intent). Non-functional
      requirements from new system.non_functional{} object with sub-fields for performance,
      scalability, security, reliability, maintainability. Renders two sub-sections with feature
      table and NFR table.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: all feature .usm files for functional requirements (summary, intent, status)
      - id: s2
        action: read
        target: system.non_functional for NFRs
      - id: s3
        action: render
        target: Functional Requirements as a table of features with descriptions
      - id: s4
        action: render
        target: Non-Functional Requirements as a table with category, requirement, target
  - id: render-system-architecture
    name: Render System Architecture page
    description: >-
      Section 3. High-level diagram from Mermaid (services + dependencies). Technology stack from
      service.tech_stack across all services. System components from system.services + system.apis.
      Renders diagram, tech stack table, and component list.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: generate
        target: high-level Mermaid diagram from system.services and depends_on
      - id: s2
        action: collect
        target: tech_stack from all services, merged by layer
      - id: s3
        action: read
        target: system.services for component list with types and ports
      - id: s4
        action: read
        target: system.apis for third-party service dependencies
      - id: s5
        action: render
        target: diagram, tech stack table, components table
  - id: render-module-design
    name: Render Module Design page
    description: >-
      Section 4. Per-module breakdown from service.modules[]. For each module: name, purpose,
      inputs, outputs, dependencies, and a flow description (from features whose
      implementation.primary is in the module). Renders one sub-section per module.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: all service .usm files for modules[]
      - id: s2
        action: match
        target: features to modules via implementation.primary path
      - id: s3
        action: render
        target: per-module sub-section with name, purpose, inputs, outputs, dependencies, flow
  - id: render-database-design
    name: Render Database Design page
    description: >-
      Section 5. ER diagram from data .usm files or detected ORM schema. Schema design from data.usm
      models. Indexes from data.usm index definitions. Transactions from data.usm transaction
      strategies. Renders ER diagram, schema tables, index list, transaction notes. Only rendered if
      data .usm files or a detected ORM schema exist.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: data .usm files for models, indexes, transactions
      - id: s2
        action: read
        target: system.data for data references
      - id: s3
        action: generate
        target: ER diagram (Mermaid) from data models
      - id: s4
        action: render
        target: schema design tables, index list, transaction notes
  - id: render-api-design
    name: Render API Design page
    description: >-
      Section 6. Endpoints from feature.routes[]. HTTP method, URL, request/response structure.
      Authentication from system.auth_schemes and service.security. Authorization from service.rbac
      (roles, resources, permissions). Rate limiting from route-level or service-level config. Error
      handling conventions. Renders endpoint table, auth summary, authz matrix, rate limiting notes,
      error format. Only rendered if any feature has routes.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: collect
        target: all feature routes[] across all services
      - id: s2
        action: read
        target: system.auth_schemes for authentication methods
      - id: s3
        action: read
        target: service.rbac for authorization model
      - id: s4
        action: read
        target: route-level or service-level rate limiting config
      - id: s5
        action: render
        target: endpoint table, auth summary, RBAC matrix, rate limits, error format
  - id: render-security-design
    name: Render Security Design page
    description: >-
      Section 7. Authentication/authorization from system.auth_schemes and service.rbac. Data
      encryption from service.security and infrastructure TLS config. Security auditing from
      system.operations and service.infrastructure.monitoring. Vulnerabilities from system.risks
      where severity is high/critical. Security stack from new system.security_stack{} field (first
      line, last line of defense). Renders auth summary, encryption table, audit notes,
      vulnerability list, security stack.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: system.auth_schemes and service.security for auth/encryption
      - id: s2
        action: read
        target: service.rbac for RBAC model
      - id: s3
        action: read
        target: service.infrastructure for TLS, secrets, monitoring
      - id: s4
        action: read
        target: system.risks filtered by severity for vulnerabilities
      - id: s5
        action: read
        target: system.security_stack for defense-in-depth layers
      - id: s6
        action: render
        target: auth summary, encryption table, audit notes, vulnerability list, security stack
  - id: render-deployment-architecture
    name: Render Deployment Architecture page
    description: >-
      Section 8. Deployment diagram (Mermaid) from system.infrastructure and service.infrastructure.
      Environment setup from system.deployment.environments (development, staging, production).
      Scaling strategy from service.infrastructure.scaling and system.infrastructure. Monitoring
      stack from system.operations and service.infrastructure.monitoring. Renders diagram,
      environment table, scaling notes, monitoring stack. Only rendered if deployment or
      infrastructure data exists.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: generate
        target: deployment Mermaid diagram from infrastructure data
      - id: s2
        action: read
        target: system.deployment.environments for environment table
      - id: s3
        action: read
        target: service.infrastructure.scaling for scaling strategy
      - id: s4
        action: read
        target: system.operations and service.infrastructure.monitoring for monitoring stack
      - id: s5
        action: render
        target: diagram, environment table, scaling notes, monitoring stack
  - id: render-testing-strategy
    name: Render Testing Strategy page
    description: >-
      Section 9. Unit testing from service.testing (framework, command, coverage target).
      Integration testing from service.testing_details. Acceptance testing from feature.tests[].
      Performance testing and security testing from new system.testing_strategy{} field. Automated
      testing from CI integration notes in system.testing_strategy. Renders per-layer testing table
      with framework, command, and notes.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: service.testing and service.testing_details for unit/integration
      - id: s2
        action: read
        target: feature.tests[] for acceptance test count and coverage
      - id: s3
        action: read
        target: system.testing_strategy for performance, security, automated testing policies
      - id: s4
        action: render
        target: >-
          testing strategy table per layer (unit, integration, acceptance, performance, security,
          automated)
  - id: render-maintenance-monitoring
    name: Render Maintenance & Monitoring page
    description: >-
      Section 10. Logging from system.operations and service.infrastructure.monitoring.logs.
      Alerting from system.operations.alerts. System health monitoring from
      service.infrastructure.monitoring.metrics. Error tracking from new system.error_tracking{}
      field. Renders logging strategy, alerting config, health metrics, error tracking tool.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: system.operations for monitoring, alerts, on-call
      - id: s2
        action: read
        target: service.infrastructure.monitoring for logs, metrics, alarms
      - id: s3
        action: read
        target: system.error_tracking for error tracking tool and config
      - id: s4
        action: render
        target: logging strategy, alerting config, health metrics table, error tracking notes
  - id: render-backup-recovery
    name: Render Backup & Recovery page
    description: >-
      Section 11. Backup strategy from service.infrastructure.data.backup_retention_days and new
      system.backup_recovery{} field. Disaster recovery from
      service.infrastructure.disaster_recovery (rto_minutes, rpo_minutes) and
      system.backup_recovery. Renders backup schedule table, RTO/RPO targets, disaster recovery
      plan. Only rendered if backup or DR data exists.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: service.infrastructure.data for backup_retention_days
      - id: s2
        action: read
        target: service.infrastructure.disaster_recovery for rto/rpo
      - id: s3
        action: read
        target: system.backup_recovery for backup strategy and DR plan
      - id: s4
        action: render
        target: backup schedule table, RTO/RPO targets, DR plan summary
  - id: render-risks-mitigation
    name: Render Risks & Mitigation page
    description: >-
      Section 12. Technical risks from system.risks[] (all items). Mitigation strategies from each
      risk's mitigation field. Also includes service-level risks from service.risks[]. Renders risk
      table with ID, title, severity, status, mitigation, and a severity summary chart.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: system.risks for system-level risks
      - id: s2
        action: read
        target: service.risks for service-level risks
      - id: s3
        action: render
        target: risk table with ID, title, severity, status, mitigation
      - id: s4
        action: render
        target: severity summary grouped by severity level
  - id: render-future-enhancements
    name: Render Future Enhancements page
    description: >-
      Section 13. Roadmap from system.roadmap[] (planned and in-progress items). Scalability
      considerations from service.infrastructure.scaling and service.future[] items. Renders roadmap
      table with status badges, target dates, and future items list grouped by service.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: read
        target: system.roadmap for roadmap items
      - id: s2
        action: read
        target: service.future for per-service future items
      - id: s3
        action: read
        target: service.infrastructure.scaling for scalability considerations
      - id: s4
        action: render
        target: roadmap table with status, target date, and feature link
      - id: s5
        action: render
        target: future items grouped by service, scalability notes
  - id: render-decision-register
    name: Render Decision Register page
    description: >-
      Consolidates all decisions[] from features, services, and system.principles into one page.
      Each decision shows ID, decision text, status, rationale, alternatives considered,
      consequences, date, and source. Renders a table view and a detailed view.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: collect
        target: decisions[] from all feature .usm files
      - id: s2
        action: collect
        target: decisions[] from all service .usm files
      - id: s3
        action: collect
        target: system.principles as architecture decisions
      - id: s4
        action: render
        target: summary table (ID, decision, status, source) and detailed per-decision breakdown
  - id: restructure-sidebar
    name: Restructure sidebar into five groups
    description: >-
      The docs sidebar generator is updated to produce five top-level groups: Getting Started (Home,
      Getting Started), Design (13 pages, only rendered ones), Project Management (Roadmap, Features
      grouped by area, Decision Register), Developers (Source Map, Test Coverage, Spec Coverage, API
      Reference, CLI Reference, Configuration), Exports (TOGAF Phases, ArchiMate Model). Groups and
      pages only appear if data exists. The existing Core Concepts, Workflows, Architecture,
      Deployment, and Contributing groups are replaced by this structure.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: determine
        target: which Design pages have data (same logic as gen-design-doc)
      - id: s2
        action: build
        target: Getting Started group from index + getting-started page
      - id: s3
        action: build
        target: Design group with only rendered section pages
      - id: s4
        action: build
        target: Project Management group with roadmap, features (grouped), decision register
      - id: s5
        action: build
        target: Developers group with source-map pages and reference pages that have data
      - id: s6
        action: build
        target: Exports group with TOGAF and ArchiMate (collapsed)
contracts:
  - id: adapt-to-project-type
    description: >-
      The generator must adapt to any project type — a CLI tool with no database, a library with no
      deployment, a monorepo with multiple services. Sections with no data are suppressed entirely,
      not rendered as empty stubs.
    must_have:
      - Each of the 13 pages is only rendered if its data source exists
      - The sidebar only includes links to rendered pages
      - A CLI-only project produces no Database Design, API Design, or Deployment Architecture pages
      - No page contains an empty section with no content
  - id: one-source-many-views
    description: >-
      The technical design document is a view of the same .usm data as TOGAF, feature docs, and
      source mapping. It must not duplicate or hardcode content — it renders from specs.
    must_have:
      - All content is derived from .usm files, schema, or declared content blocks
      - No USM-specific content hardcoded in the generator
      - >-
        No content duplicated from TOGAF or feature docs — both read from the same specs
        independently
  - id: content-block-enrichment
    description: >-
      system.usm can declare inline content blocks for any Design section via a new design_pages[]
      field (parallel to reference_pages[]). These enrich the structured data with prose that
      doesn't fit schema fields.
    must_have:
      - >-
        design_pages[] entries have id, title, audience, and either content[] (content blocks) or
        source (structured data)
      - Inline content blocks render AFTER structured data on the same page
      - Sections without design_pages entries still render from structured data alone
  - id: prose-escaping
    description: >-
      All prose from specs must be escaped via escapeProse before rendering to prevent VitePress Vue
      template compilation errors from unescaped angle brackets.
    must_have:
      - All spec-authored prose passed through escapeProse
      - All table cell content passed through escapeTableCell or escCell
      - Mermaid diagram blocks are exempt (they use their own escaping)
  - id: sidebar-data-driven
    description: >-
      The sidebar structure is generated from data availability, not hardcoded. Groups and pages
      appear only when their underlying data exists.
    must_have:
      - Design group only includes pages that were rendered
      - Project Management group only appears if roadmap, features, or decisions exist
      - Developers group only includes reference pages that have data
      - Exports group only appears if TOGAF or ArchiMate outputs exist
  - id: decision-register
    description: >-
      The Decision Register consolidates all decisions from features, services, and system
      principles into one page — the single source of truth for what was decided and why.
    must_have:
      - All feature decisions[] included with source feature $id
      - All service decisions[] included with source service $id
      - System principles[] rendered as architecture decisions
      - Each decision shows ID, decision, status, rationale, alternatives, consequences
  - id: nav-template-structure
    description: |-
      The generated sidebar follows a fixed five-group template structure that applies to any
      project. The template defines which groups exist, which pages go in each group, and which
      content sections each Design page contains. Groups and pages are suppressed when their data
      source doesn't exist, but the structure itself is the standard template.
    must_have:
      - "Getting Started group: Home, Getting Started"
      - >-
        Design group: 13 section pages (Project Overview, Requirements, System Architecture, Module
        Design, Database Design, API Design, Security Design, Deployment Architecture, Testing
        Strategy, Maintenance & Monitoring, Backup & Recovery, Risks & Mitigation, Future
        Enhancements) — only rendered pages appear
      - "Project Management group: Roadmap, Features (grouped by service/area), Decision Register"
      - >-
        Developers group: Source Map, Test Coverage, Spec Coverage, API Reference, CLI Reference,
        Configuration (only pages with data appear)
      - "Exports group: TOGAF Phases, ArchiMate Model (collapsed)"
      - >-
        Each Design page has a defined set of content sections (3rd-level outline) that the
        generator renders — these are the page's TOC, not separate sidebar links
tests:
  - id: design-full-system
    setup:
      system_with_services_features_data: true
    expect:
      - assertion: 13 design pages generated when all data sources present
      - assertion: Each page has frontmatter with title and generated date
      - assertion: All prose is escaped (no raw < or > outside code fences and Mermaid blocks)
      - assertion: Sidebar Design group contains links to all 13 rendered pages
  - id: design-cli-only
    setup:
      system_with_cli_service_only: true
    expect:
      - assertion: No Database Design page generated (no data .usm files)
      - assertion: No Deployment Architecture page generated (no infrastructure data)
      - assertion: Sidebar Design group only includes pages that were rendered
      - assertion: No empty stub pages with zero content
  - id: design-content-block-enrichment
    setup:
      system_with_design_pages_content_blocks: true
    expect:
      - assertion: Inline content blocks render after structured data on the same page
      - assertion: Sections without design_pages entries still render from structured data
      - assertion: Content blocks use the same content-block renderer as reference_pages
  - id: decision-register
    setup:
      features_and_services_with_decisions: true
    expect:
      - assertion: All feature decisions included with source $id
      - assertion: All service decisions included with source $id
      - assertion: System principles rendered as architecture decisions
      - assertion: Each decision shows ID, decision, status, rationale
  - id: sidebar-data-driven
    setup:
      system_with_partial_data: true
    expect:
      - assertion: Sidebar groups only appear when their data exists
      - assertion: Design group pages match exactly what was rendered
      - assertion: No dead links to pages that don't exist
  - id: nav-template-conformance
    setup:
      system_with_all_data_sources: true
    expect:
      - assertion: >-
          Sidebar has exactly five groups in order (Getting Started, Design, Project Management,
          Developers, Exports)
      - assertion: Design group contains all 13 section pages when all data present
      - assertion: Project Management group contains Roadmap, Features, Decision Register
      - assertion: >-
          Developers group contains Source Map, Test Coverage, Spec Coverage, API Reference, CLI
          Reference, Configuration
      - assertion: Exports group is collapsed and contains TOGAF Phases, ArchiMate Model
  - id: prose-escaping
    setup:
      spec_with_angle_brackets_in_descriptions: true
    expect:
      - assertion: All prose escaped via escapeProse — no raw < or > outside code/Mermaid
      - assertion: Table cells escaped via escapeTableCell — no raw < or > or unescaped pipes
      - assertion: VitePress build succeeds without template compilation errors
decisions:
  - id: generator-not-author
    decision: >-
      The technical design document is a generator (structured renderer), not an authored doc. All
      content comes from .usm specs, schema, or declared content blocks.
    rationale: >-
      Follows the first principle established by gen-content-blocks: generators are renderers, not
      authors. The mapping from USM data to the 13 sections is deterministic.
  - id: design-pages-parallel-to-reference-pages
    decision: >-
      system.usm gains a design_pages[] field parallel to reference_pages[]. Each entry can declare
      inline content blocks for a section, enriching the structured data.
    rationale: >-
      Some sections need prose that doesn't fit structured fields (e.g. disaster recovery narrative,
      testing philosophy). Content blocks provide that escape hatch while keeping the generator as
      the primary renderer.
  - id: suppress-empty-sections
    decision: >-
      Sections with no data are suppressed entirely — no empty stub pages, no sidebar links to pages
      that don't exist.
    rationale: >-
      Follows the docs-experience principle: a CLI tool shouldn't have a Database Design page. The
      template adapts to the project.
  - id: five-group-sidebar
    decision: >-
      The sidebar is restructured into five groups: Getting Started, Design, Project Management,
      Developers, Exports. This replaces the current fragmented groups (Core Concepts, Workflows,
      Architecture, Deployment, Contributing).
    rationale: >-
      The current sidebar mixes concerns — architecture phases next to reference pages next to
      feature groups. Five clean groups by audience and purpose: onboarding, holistic design,
      project governance, developer reference, alternative framework exports.
  - id: nav-template-definition
    decision: |-
      The nav template structure is the standard for all generated technical docs. It defines five
      top-level groups and the pages within each. The template is fixed in structure but adaptive in
      rendering — groups and pages only appear when data exists.

      ```
      Getting Started
        Home
        Getting Started

      Design
        Project Overview
          Project Name
          Project Description
          Stakeholders
          Assumptions
          Use-cases
        Requirements
          Functional Requirements
          Non-Functional Requirements
            Performance
            Scalability
            Security
            Reliability
            Maintainability
        System Architecture
          High-Level Diagram
          Technology Stack
            Frontend
            Backend
            Database
            Infrastructure
          System Components
        Module Design
          Module Name
          Purpose
          Inputs
          Outputs
          Dependencies
          Flow
        Database Design
          ER Diagram
          Schema Design
          Indexes
          Transactions
        API Design
          Endpoints
            HTTP Method (GET/POST/PUT/DELETE)
            URL Schemas / naming conventions
          Request/Response structure
          Authentication
          Authorization (roles, resources, permissions)
          Rate Limiting
          Error Handling
        Security Design
          Authentication / Authorization
          Data Encryption
          Security Auditing
          Vulnerabilities
          Security Stack
        Deployment Architecture
          Deployment Diagram
          Environment Setup
            Development
            Staging
            Production
          Scaling Strategy
          Monitoring Stack
        Testing Strategy
          Unit Testing
          Integration Testing
          Acceptance Testing
          Performance Testing
          Security Testing
          Automated Testing
        Maintenance & Monitoring
          Logging
          Alerting
          System Health Monitoring
          Error Tracking
        Backup & Recovery
          Backup Strategy
          Disaster Recovery
        Risks & Mitigation
          Technical Risks
          Mitigation Strategies
        Future Enhancements
          Roadmap
          Scalability Considerations

      Project Management
        Roadmap
        Features (grouped by service/area)
        Decision Register

      Developers
        Source Map
        Test Coverage
        Spec Coverage
        API Reference
        CLI Reference
        Configuration

      Exports (collapsed)
        TOGAF Phases
        ArchiMate Model
      ```

      Level 1 = sidebar group. Level 2 = sidebar page link. Level 3+ = page content
      sections (rendered as the page outline/TOC, not sidebar links). The sidebar is 2
      levels deep (group > page). Groups and pages only appear when their data source
      exists — the template adapts to the project type.
    rationale: >-
      Capturing the full template structure as a decision ensures the generator has a single
      authoritative reference for what to render. The template is universal — it applies to any
      project type — but adapts by suppressing sections without data.
  - id: decision-register-consolidated
    decision: >-
      A Decision Register page consolidates all decisions from features, services, and system
      principles into one page under Project Management.
    rationale: >-
      Decisions are currently scattered across feature docs and TOGAF Phase H. A single register is
      the project governance view — what was decided, why, and what alternatives were rejected.
  - id: features-as-project-management
    decision: >-
      Features are grouped under Project Management, not Design. Features with
      flows/contracts/tests/status are work records, not design prose.
    rationale: >-
      Separating how the system is designed from how the project is managed is cleaner. Design is
      the system; Project Management is the work.
implementation:
  primary: src/generators/technicalDesign.ts
  test_code: tests/technicalDesign.test.ts
  test_code_status: manual
